TI  - Meta-CAMAF: A meta-learning enhanced context-aware multimodal alignment framework for explainable risk stratification in lung diseases
T2  - ARRAY
LA  - English
KW  - Meta-learning
KW  - Multimodal fusion
KW  - Vision transformer
KW  - Clinical decision support
KW  - Lung disease stratification
AB  - Lung diseases remain a critical global health challenge, requiring accurate and trustworthy risk stratification to support clinical decision-making. While multimodal deep learning has shown promise, existing models often struggle with heterogeneous data integration, rare disease generalization, and interpretability for end-users. To address these challenges, we propose Meta-CAMAF, a meta-learning enhanced context-aware multimodal alignment framework that integrates TabTransformer for electronic health records and Vision Transformer (ViT) for chest X-rays. A context encoder and dynamic alignment layer generate patient-specific representations, while a meta-context gating mechanism enables rapid adaptation to rare disease cases in few-shot settings. Beyond predictive accuracy, Meta-CAMAF provides attention-based interpretability for visualizing modality contributions and highlighting clinically relevant features, strengthening its role as a decision-support tool. Evaluations on CheXpert, MIMIC-IV, and PadChest datasets show state-of-the-art performance with an accuracy of 0.9736, F1-score of 0.9698, and AUC-ROC of 0.9864, significantly outperforming recent multimodal and meta-learning baselines. Robustness analyses under noise, calibration diagnostics, out-of-distribution detection, and statistical significance testing confirm the reliability and clinical viability of the framework. Meta-CAMAF thus offers a scalable and explainable solution for precision healthcare, bridging technical advances in multimodal learning with real-world clinical deployment needs.
