TI  - AUC-Net: an alignment-aware gating framework for robust uncertainty calibration in medical image segmentation
T2  - EXPERT SYSTEMS WITH APPLICATIONS
LA  - English
KW  - Medical image segmentation
KW  - Out-of-distribution robustness
KW  - Probabilistic calibration
KW  - Intermediate-perception attention
KW  - Feature-semantic misalignment
AB  - High-quality segmentation and reliable uncertainty estimation are both essential for trustworthy medical image analysis, yet existing methods typically treat them as loosely coupled objectives. In this work, we revisit this paradigm and identify a structural limitation in U-shaped architectures, namely the feature-semantic misalignment, where intermediate representations simultaneously encode spatial details and evolving semantic abstractions, leading to inconsistent confidence estimation and degraded calibration under distribution shifts. To address this issue, we propose AUC-Net, a principled framework that explicitly models the interaction between representation alignment and uncertainty estimation. Unlike conventional attention mechanisms that primarily enhance feature saliency, we introduce the Intermediate-Perception Attention Module (IPAM), which performs top-down semantic-conditioned reweighting of mid-level features, enabling progressive alignment between categorical semantics and spatial structures. Building upon this aligned representation, we further develop a dual-objective uncertainty formulation that integrates evidential deep learning with a topology-aware segmentation loss. Specifically, a Dirichlet-based evidential formulation captures data uncertainty, while a Softplussmoothed Log-Dice term regularizes boundary consistency, jointly improving both prediction reliability and structural fidelity. Extensive experiments on four benchmark datasets (ISIC2018, ACDC, LiTS2017, and MVSeg-3D TEE) demonstrate that AUC-Net achieves consistent improvements in calibration and robustness, particularly under out-of-distribution perturbations. These results suggest that explicitly addressing representation-level misalignment provides a complementary pathway to improving both segmentation accuracy and uncertainty reliability. The code will be available at https://github.com/zhangjy2008327/AUC-Net.
